(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["cam-nutrition-exam-1-human-nutrition-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Human Nutrition (Set 2) — CAM/Nutrition Exam 1","category":"CAM / Nutrition","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which of the following is required for a nutrient to be classified as truly 'essential' per the lecture's definition?","choices":["It must be synthesized by the liver in amounts sufficient to meet the body's entire daily requirement","Omitting it from the diet must cause a decline in function that is restored when it's added back","It must provide at least 4 kcal per gram, the same energy yield used to classify a true macronutrient","It must taste pleasant to most people, since palatability was described as the top driver of food choice"],"answer":1,"exp":"An essential nutrient has one specific biological function identified in the body, its omission from the diet leads to a decline in that function, and replacing it restores normal function again — that three-part test is exactly what the lecture lays out. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 5 (Nutrition / Essential Nutrient)"},{"q":"Fiber is classified within which of the lecture's six classes of nutrients?","choices":["Amino acid","Trace metal","Carbohydrate","Fatty acid"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lecture lists glycogen, starch, and fiber together as complex carbohydrates, so fiber falls under the carbohydrate class of nutrients. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 13 (Carbohydrates)"},{"q":"According to the lecture, what is the actual difference between a 'calorie' (lowercase c) and a 'kilocalorie' (kcal)?","choices":["A calorie measures only the energy content of fat, while a kcal measures only carbohydrate energy","They are different names for the exact same amount of heat energy, used interchangeably in lecture and on food labels","A calorie raises the temperature of 1 g of water 1°C, while a kcal raises 1,000 g (1 L) of water 1°C","A kcal is 1/1000 the size of a calorie, making the calorie the larger of the two heat-energy units used in lecture"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lecture defines calorie (little c) as the heat needed to raise 1 g of water 1°C, and kilocalorie as the heat needed to raise 1,000 g (1 L) of water 1°C — 1 kcal equals 1,000 calories. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 22 (Calories Defined)"},{"q":"Which characteristic distinguishes minerals from vitamins per the lecture?","choices":["Minerals are organic, carbon-containing compounds like vitamins","Minerals are inorganic substances that do not contain carbon atoms","Minerals provide 4 kcal per gram, same as carbohydrate and protein","Minerals are destroyed during cooking, unlike most trace elements"],"answer":1,"exp":"The lecture defines minerals as inorganic substances that do not contain carbon atoms, unlike vitamins, which are organic compounds. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 19 (Minerals), contrast slide 18 (Vitamins)"},{"q":"In the lecture's Grilled Chicken Sandwich example, the 45 grams of protein contributed how many of the sandwich's 490 total kcal?","choices":["180 kcal","225 kcal","405 kcal","130 kcal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Protein provides ~4 kcal/gram, so 45 g × 4 = 180 kcal, matching the lecture's worked example exactly. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 23 (Calculating Calories)"},{"q":"In the lecture's Grilled Chicken Sandwich example (490 total kcal), which macronutrient contributed the most kcal even though it does not have the highest kcal-per-gram rate?","choices":["Dietary alcohol","Dietary protein","Dietary fat grams","Carbohydrate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Carbohydrate contributed 184 kcal (46 g × 4 kcal/g) — more than fat's 126 kcal (14 g × 9 kcal/g) or protein's 180 kcal (45 g × 4 kcal/g) — even though fat's kcal/g rate is more than double carbohydrate's, because there were far more grams of carbohydrate present. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 23 (Calculating Calories)"},{"q":"On a nutrition facts food label, the 'Calories' figure (capital C) is numerically equivalent to which unit discussed in the lecture?","choices":["kilocalorie (kcal)","calorie (lowercase c)","kilogram","joule"],"answer":0,"exp":"The lecture states that energy on food labels is expressed as Calories (capital C), which is the same as kcal — 1,000 calories = 1 kcal = 1 (food) Calorie. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 22 (Calories Defined)"},{"q":"Using the lecture's metric conversions, approximately how many milliliters are in one cup?","choices":["240 mL","30 mL","946 mL","5 mL"],"answer":0,"exp":"The lecture states 1 cup is about 240 milliliters. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 27 (Metric System Summary)"},{"q":"A patient weighs 176 pounds. Using the lecture's conversion (divide pounds by 2.2), approximately how many kilograms is this?","choices":["40 kg","176 kg","387 kg","80 kg"],"answer":3,"exp":"176 lb ÷ 2.2 = 80 kg, following the lecture's stated conversion method exactly. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 26 (Metric System Summary (1))"},{"q":"According to the lecture's metric summary, one pound (lb) is equivalent to how many grams?","choices":["1,000 grams","100 grams","28 grams","454 grams"],"answer":3,"exp":"The lecture states one pound (lb) equals 454 grams. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 26 (Metric System Summary (1))"},{"q":"Per the lecture's metric summary, which statement correctly compares teaspoon-equivalents of sugar and salt?","choices":["4 g of salt and 6 g of sugar are each about one teaspoon","Teaspoon-equivalents were not given for sugar or salt","4 g of sugar and 6 g of salt are each about one teaspoon","Sugar and salt both equal exactly 5 g per teaspoon"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lecture states 4 grams of sugar is about 1 teaspoon, and separately that 6 grams of salt is about 1 teaspoon — different gram weights for the same volume because the substances have different densities. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 26 (Metric System Summary (1))"},{"q":"A patient with a BMI of 32 falls into which category per the lecture's BMI guidelines?","choices":["Healthy body weight","Class 1 Obesity","Class 2 Obesity","Overweight"],"answer":1,"exp":"The lecture's BMI guidelines place 30–34.99 in the Class 1 Obesity category, and 32 falls squarely within that range. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 29 (Obesity Definition / BMI Guidelines)"},{"q":"Per the 2018 CDC statistics cited in the lecture, approximately what percentage of the US population was classified as obese?","choices":["72.5%","90%","41%","26%"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lecture cites 41% of the US population as obese in the 2018 CDC statistics. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 30 (2018 CDC stats on obesity)"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed among obesity's consequences in the lecture?","choices":["Improved joint mobility","Lost work productivity","Decreased cardiovascular disease risk","Increased bone density"],"answer":1,"exp":"The lecture lists lost work productivity as an economic burden of obesity, alongside chronic diseases like cardiovascular disease, hypertension, diabetes, kidney failure, arthritis/joint failure, and some cancers. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 31 (Obesity's Consequences)"},{"q":"Per the NHANES data cited in the lecture, which macronutrient's actual 2018 intake percentage slightly exceeded the top of its advised range?","choices":["Fat","Protein","Carbohydrate","Alcohol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Fat's actual intake was 35.8% of kcal, just above the 20–35% advised range cited in the lecture — the one macronutrient whose real intake edged past its recommended ceiling. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 32 (Assessing the Current North American Eating Pattern)"},{"q":"Which dietary change does the lecture actually recommend for improving the current North American eating pattern?","choices":["Choose more nutrient-dense foods","Choose animal proteins over plant proteins","Choose solid fats over plant oils","Decrease the quantity and variety of fruits and vegetables"],"answer":0,"exp":"The lecture recommends choosing more nutrient-dense foods as one of several improvements needed in the current eating pattern. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 33 (Assessing the Current North American Diet)"},{"q":"Which set of proportions correctly describes the MyPlate icon referenced in the lecture?","choices":["All five food groups occupy four equal quarters of the plate, split evenly with no group given priority","Protein covers half the plate, with grains and fruits/vegetables splitting the remaining two fourths evenly","Grains cover half the plate, with protein and fruits/vegetables splitting the remaining two fourths evenly","Fruits/vegetables cover half the plate, grains about one-fourth, protein about one-fourth, with a cup of dairy alongside"],"answer":3,"exp":"The lecture describes fruits and vegetables covering half the plate, grains/starches occupying about one-fourth, protein occupying the remaining one-fourth, and a cup of dairy appearing alongside the plate. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 57 (Dishing Up MyPlate)"},{"q":"Per the lecture, which factor is ranked as the #1 influence on food choice?","choices":["Environmental sustainability","Marketing and advertising","Overall cost and economics","Flavor, texture, and appearance"],"answer":3,"exp":"The lecture explicitly ranks flavor, texture, and appearance as the #1 influence on food choice, ahead of the other listed influences. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 3 (Food Choice Influences)"},{"q":"The lecture highlights tomatoes as a source of which phytochemical, used as an example of a functional food?","choices":["Anthocyanin","Allicin","Lycopene","Beta-carotene"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lecture specifically names lycopene as the phytochemical in tomatoes that is important for health, used to introduce the concept of functional foods. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 45 (Functional Foods)"},{"q":"The lecture notes that increased affluence in North America has been linked to which dietary and lifestyle shift?","choices":["A universal, exclusive improvement in nutrient density seen equally across every income level","Higher intake of animal fat, sugar, salt, and alcohol, alongside more sedentary lifestyles","A complete shift away from convenience and processed foods toward home-cooked, whole-food meals","Reduced intake of animal fat, sugar, salt, and alcohol across nearly every demographic group"],"answer":1,"exp":"The lecture states that although North Americans generally live longer and have more money, diverse food, and lifestyle choices, affluence has also led to sedentary lifestyles and high intakes of animal fat, sugar, salt, and alcohol. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 42 (Longer Healthier Lives)"},{"q":"How does the lecture distinguish simple from complex carbohydrates?","choices":["Simple carbohydrates are monosaccharides and disaccharides; complex carbohydrates include glycogen, starch, and fiber","Simple carbohydrates provide 9 kilocalories per gram and complex provide 4","Simple carbohydrates are inorganic and complex are organic","Complex carbohydrates cannot be used for energy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: simple carbohydrates are mono- and disaccharides, while complex carbohydrates are glycogen, starch, and fiber. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 13"},{"q":"According to the lecture, how does typical North American protein intake compare with the body's needs?","choices":["Protein provides about 9 kilocalories per gram","Most North Americans eat up to two times as much protein as the body needs to maintain health","Most North Americans eat far less protein than needed","Protein intake exactly matches the body's needs on average"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the lecture notes most North Americans eat up to twice the protein the body needs. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 14"},{"q":"How does the lecture contrast animal fats with plant oils?","choices":["Both animal fats and plant oils provide 4 kilocalories per gram","Plant oils raise blood cholesterol more than animal fats","Animal fats are solid at room temperature; plant oils are liquid and contain essential fatty acids","Animal fats are liquid and plant oils are solid at room temperature"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: animal fats are solid and should be limited, while plant oils are liquid and supply essential fatty acids. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 15"},{"q":"Per the metric conversions in the lecture, about how many grams are in one pound?","choices":["About 1,000 grams","About 100 grams","About 2.2 grams","About 454 grams"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: one pound is about 454 grams. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 26"},{"q":"According to the lecture's metric summary, about how many milliliters are in one cup?","choices":["About 240 milliliters","About 5 milliliters","About 1,000 milliliters","About 30 milliliters"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: one cup is about 240 milliliters. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 27"},{"q":"What alcohol-moderation limit does the lecture give for health promotion?","choices":["Up to two drinks per day for women and four for men","No more than two drinks per day for men and one drink per day for women","No more than five drinks per day for anyone","No alcohol is permitted under any circumstances"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the moderation guidance is up to two drinks per day for men and one for women. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 40"},{"q":"What weekly physical-activity target does the lecture cite for health promotion?","choices":["500 minutes per week","No specific target is provided","150 minutes per week","30 minutes per week"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the lecture cites at least 30–60 minutes on most days for a total of 150 minutes per week. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 40"},{"q":"According to the lecture, what training does a Registered Dietitian complete?","choices":["A weekend certification course only","A medical degree and residency in surgery","No formal training or credentialing","A four-year degree, usually a post-graduate internship, and a national exam through the Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: a Registered Dietitian completes a four-year degree, typically an internship, and the national Registered Dietitian exam. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 61"},{"q":"How does the lecture distinguish nutrient density from energy density?","choices":["Nutrient density compares a food's nutrient content to its calories; energy density compares a food's calorie content to its weight","Both terms mean the total calories in a serving","Nutrient density is calories per gram and energy density is nutrients per gram","Energy density measures the vitamins in a food"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: nutrient density is nutrients per calorie, while energy density is calories per weight of food. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 51"},{"q":"Which set of chronic conditions does the lecture link to obesity?","choices":["Vitamin toxicity and dehydration","Cardiovascular disease, hypertension, diabetes, kidney failure, joint problems, and some cancers","Only temporary fatigue with no chronic disease risk","Improved cardiovascular and joint health"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the lecture ties obesity to cardiovascular disease, hypertension, diabetes, kidney failure, arthritis/joint failure, and some cancers. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 31"}]};
